[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Assertions and Soft Assertions in JUnit"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Assertions are the backbone of automated testing. They allow you to validate that the actual results of your code match the expected results. In JUnit 5, assertions are primarily handled by the org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions class."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Hard Assertions (Standard Assertions)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, JUnit uses hard assertions. When a hard assertion fails, the test execution stops immediately at that line. Any subsequent assertions in the same test method are skipped. This is useful when subsequent checks depend on the previous check passing."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

public class HardAssertionTest {
    @Test
    void testHardAssertions() {
        assertEquals(10, 5 + 5, "Math is broken");
        assertTrue(true, "Should be true");
        // If the line above fails, execution stops here
        assertNotNull("JUnit", "Should not be null");
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Soft Assertions Using assertAll"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Often, you want to execute all validation checks in a test method, even if some of them fail. This is where soft assertions come in. JUnit provides the assertAll method, which groups multiple assertions together and reports all failures at once."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

public class SoftAssertionTest {
    @Test
    void testGroupedAssertions() {
        assertAll("User Validation",
            () -> assertEquals("John", "Jane", "First name does not match"),
            () -> assertEquals(30, 25, "Age does not match"),
            () -> assertTrue(false, "Status should be active")
        );
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"How assertAll Works"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"It accepts an optional heading description string followed by multiple Executable lambdas."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Every lambda inside assertAll is executed, regardless of whether preceding assertions fail."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"If one or more assertions fail, a MultipleFailuresError is thrown at the end, listing all the individual failures."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use hard assertions when subsequent tests or logic depend entirely on the current state being correct."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use assertAll when validating complex objects or UI forms where you want to see all validation errors simultaneously instead of fixing them one by one."}]
